  "title": "How the git guard judges one shell call",
  "mechanism": "The guard splits one shell call into its commands, finds the repo each runs in, judges each by that repo's rules, and the strictest verdict decides.",
  "lens": "transformation",
  "claim": "A harmless-looking command can't carry a forbidden one: each git call is judged where it really runs, and one deny stops the line.",
  "implication": "Chaining with cd, git -C or bash -c is no way round a rule, and each repo keeps its own settings.",
